age_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be an int >= 32 or None, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n            if max_message_chars < _MIN_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\n\nNote: bool check must come before int check since bool is int.\n\nformat method: need to truncate the message after %-substitution, before prefixing/color. Implementation approach: get `record.getMessage()` — but careful not to mutate record. Approach:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        if budget is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = _truncate_message(message, budget)\n        # 计算前缀，用 trunc 后的 message 替换 record.message\n        ...\n```\n\nCareful: super().format(record) uses record.message (set by super().format via record.getMessage()) and appends exc_text/stack_info. Also uses record.msg? Formatter.format sets record.message = record.getMessage(), then s = self.formatMessage(record) which uses record.message. So I need to control record.message without changing msg/args. Trick: call `super().format(record)` first? That would format the full message including exception text, and then I'd need to truncate only the message portion inside. That's messy but doable: after super().format, msg = full string; prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)] — original code already does that for newline alignment. But truncating inside the formatted string risks truncating exception text positions... Actually we can truncate before calling super().format by temporarily... no mutation allowed? We cannot rewrite record.msg/args, but record.message is set by base format anyway. Mutating record.message would be a leftover but the record is shared with full handler which calls format again, re-setting record.message via getMessage. But safer: set record.message to truncated, call super().format, then restore original record.message? Also exc_text caching: base Formatter caches record.exc_text — but that only affects exception text, identical for both handlers; fine.\n\nBetter clean approach: compute truncated message, temporarily set record.message = truncated and format manually? Actually cleanest: use a shallow copy of the record? But record.message then not set on original — fine since base format sets message on the passed record; if we pass a copy, the original record's message stays unset (or as set previously), and the full handler will set it itself. Copy is safe: `copy.copy(record)`. But `copy` module import — allowed? Imports allowed list includes only listed stdlib modules for action code; but the file's own imports — the restriction is on my action code imports. logger.py already imports datetime, logging, etc. Adding `import copy`... does the restriction \"Imports allowed: [...]\" apply to my action code only, or to the target file? It says \"Imports allowed: [...]\" for action code. Adding new stdlib import to logger.py is permitted by task (\"可增加必要标准库导入\"). But to be safe, avoid copy: temporarily set record.message, restore after.\n\nBut careful: original format code uses record.message for newline alignment. Plan:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        original_message = record.message if hasattr(record, \"message\") else None\n        ...\n```\n\nSimpler: implement format as:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        if budget is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            truncated = _truncate_message(message, budget)\n            if truncated != message:\n                saved = record.__dict__.get(\"message\")\n                record.message = truncated\n                try:\n                    msg = super().format(record)\n                finally:\n                    if saved is None:\n                        record.__dict__.pop(\"message\", None)\n                    else:\n                        record.message = saved\n                return self._decorate(msg, record)\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        return self._decorate(msg, record)\n```\n\nHmm but the newline-prefix alignment uses record.message which is the truncated one — consistent. But wait, if we restore record.message after, the \"full\" handler will call getMessage separately, fine.\n\nActually there's a subtlety: base Formatter.format sets record.message = record.getMessage() at start, so saved value restoration leaves original record.message (whatever). Fine.\n\nWait but also exc_text caching: base format caches record.exc_text = self.formatException(...). That's fine.\n\nAlternatively simpler: call base format on truncated copy without restoring... but must not leave mutated record for the subsequent full handler? Full handler's format() calls record.getMessage() again and overwrites message, so no issue except record.message left truncated in between — but the full handler recomputes. Still restoring is safer, and test may check record.msg/args not mutated (message isn't in the forbidden list, but let's restore anyway).\n\nHowever, restoring via pop when saved is None: careful if `\"message\"` in record.__dict__ originally. Handle both.\n\nHmm, but the decorate (newline prefix + color) — original code did it inside format. I'll keep one code path:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        keep_full = getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True\n        if budget is None or keep_full:\n            msg = super().format(record)\n        else:\n            full_message = record.getMessage()\n            truncated = _truncate_message(full_message, budget)\n            if truncated == full_message:\n                msg = super().format(record)\n            else:\n                ... temporarily swap, msg = super().format(record), restore\n```\n\nWait: `getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True` — for `1`, `1 is True` is False. Good. For string \"True\": False. Good. But careful: logging's extra with value True works.\n\nNote: getattr on a LogRecord with keep_full_message=True set via extra is fine.\n\nHelper functions:\n\n```python\n_MIN_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = 32\n_OMITTED_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\n\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, max_chars: int) -> str:\n    if len(message) <= max_chars:\n        return message\n    remaining = max_chars - len(_OMITTED_MARKER)\n    if remaining <= 0:\n        return _OMITTED_MARKER[:max_chars]\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return f\"{message[:head]}{_OMITTED_MARKER}{message[len(message) - tail:] if tail else ''}\"\n```\n\nCheck budget 32: marker len 15, remaining 17, head = 9, tail = 8. Matches example (开头9, 末尾8, 15标记 = 32). \n\nTail slice when tail > 0: message[len(message)-tail:]. Good.\n\nThen _setup_logger:\n\n```python\n    fmt = NewLineFormatter(\n        _FORMAT,\n        datefmt=_DATE_FORMAT,\n        use_color=sys.stdout.isatty(),\n        max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS,\n    )\n```\n\nNeed import: `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. Does logger.py import environ already? Lines 1-33 show imports; no environ import. Check for circular import: environ.py imports os, threading, etc. — no deepinfer imports. So safe.\n\nBut careful: repeated init reads config each time — since cached, fine.\n\nAlso note global import of envs at module import triggers nothing (descriptor access lazy).\n\n3. stats_logger.py: change `logger.info(m) ` to `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nWait, the consumer loop is in `_log_worker`. The task says \"只调整现有队列消费函数内部的日志调用\". Yes.\n\nLet me verify environ.py file length (221 lines shown, that's full? `read_range` 1..221 was in evidence via ev-22... which is the full read registration). Let's confirm total lines: The file ends with rearm_all at line 221 presumably. Good.\n\nNow let me do the edits. First environ.py.\n\nAlso `_MIN_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS` in environ.py: the min value 32 — define in environ helper with minimum=32 param.\n\nLet me write edits with replace_text_batch.\n\nenviron.py edit 1: add factory method after int_or_none.\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none() -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        return EnvVar(lambda v: int(v) if v is not None else None)\n```\n\nold_text:\n```\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none() -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        return EnvVar(lambda v: int(v) if v is not None else None)\n```\nnew_text: add min_at_least method:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none() -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        return EnvVar(lambda v: int(v) if v is not None else None)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_at_least(*, minimum: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        \"\"\"未设置时为 None；设置时须为可解析的 int 且不小于 minimum，否则 ValueError。\"\"\"\n\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{v!r} must be >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nedit 2: add the config item under Logging:\n\nold:\n```\n    # -- Logging --\n    DEEPINFER_LOGGING_INTERVAL_STEP = EnvVar.int(default=100)\n```\nnew:\n```\n    # -- Logging --\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_at_least(minimum=32)\n    \"\"\"普通诊断日志消息正文的最大字符数；未设置表示不限长，设置时须 >= 32。\"\"\"\n    DEEPINFER_LOGGING_INTERVAL_STEP = EnvVar.int(default=100)\n```\n\nNow logger.py edits. Need the exact current text of class body to replace. I have lines 33-68 evidence with content. Let me use exact strings.\n\nReplace 1 (class header + __init__ signature and body):\n\nold:\n```\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        fmt: str,\n        datefmt: str | None = None,\n        high_precision: bool = False,\n        use_color: bool = False,\n    ) -> None:\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n```\nnew:\n```\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        fmt: str,\n        datefmt: str | None = None,\n        high_precision: bool = False,\n        use_color: bool = False,\n        *,\n        max_message_chars: int | None = None,\n    ) -> None:\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be an int >= {_MIN_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS} or None, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n            if max_message_chars < _MIN_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS:\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be >= {_MIN_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS} or None, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\n\nReplace 2: format method.\n\nold:\n```\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        # 快速路径：单行日志（绝大多数情况）无需做对齐处理\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:\n            prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]\n            msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n        if self.use_color:\n            color = _LEVEL_COLORS.get(record.levelno)\n            if color:\n                msg = f\"{color}{msg}{_COLOR_RESET}\"\n        return msg\n```\nnew:\n```\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        if budget is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            full_message = record.getMessage()\n            truncated = _truncate_message(full_message, budget)\n            if truncated != full_message:\n                msg = self._format_truncated(record, truncated)\n            else:\n                msg = super().format(record)\n        else:\n            msg = super().format(record)\n        # 快速路径：单行日志（绝大多数情况）无需做对齐处理\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:\n            prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]\n            msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n        if self.use_color:\n            color = _LEVEL_COLORS.get(record.levelno)\n            if color:\n                msg = f\"{color}{msg}{_COLOR_RESET}\"\n        return msg\n\n    def _format_truncated(self, record: logging.LogRecord, truncated: str) -> str:\n        \"\"\"用截断后的正文格式化，不修改共享记录的 msg/args/exc_info/stack_info。\"\"\"\n        missing = object()\n        saved = record.__dict__.get(\"message\", missing)\n        record.message = truncated\n        try:\n            msg = super().format(record)\n        finally:\n            if saved is missing:\n                record.__dict__.pop(\"message\", None)\n            else:\n                record.message = saved\n        return msg\n```\n\nHmm wait: newline alignment after restoring: `record.message` restored to original (probably full message if previously set, or missing). In truncated branch, we restore to original, so `record.message` may not be the truncated message → alignment prefix computed via msg.find(record.message) could fail (-1) if record.message missing → `prefix = msg[: -1]`... problem if record.message is missing → `record.message` raises AttributeError? Actually `record.message` attribute access on LogRecord — LogRecord has no `message` attribute by default; accessing raises AttributeError. Original code has `and record.message` which would raise AttributeError if missing... but super().format always sets record.message, so it exists at that point. In truncated branch after restore, if saved was missing we popped it → AttributeError in the alignment check!\n\nSolution: do the alignment inside using truncated message properly. Better: capture the message used for formatting in a local variable and use it for the prefix computation.\n\nLet me restructure:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        message: str | None = None\n        if budget is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            full_message = record.getMessage()\n            truncated = _truncate_message(full_message, budget)\n            if truncated != full_message:\n                message = truncated\n                msg = self._format_truncated(record, truncated)\n            else:\n                msg = super().format(record)\n        else:\n            msg = super().format(record)\n        if message is None:\n            message = getattr(record, \"message\", None)\n        # 快速路径：单行日志（绝大多数情况）无需做对齐处理\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and message:\n            prefix = msg[: msg.find(message)]\n            msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n        ...\n```\n\nThat keeps behavior identical in the normal case (record.message just set by super().format) and uses truncated message in truncated case. Good.\n\nBut careful: if record.message appears multiple times... same as original behavior.\n\nAlso `_format_truncated` mixing: after super().format(record), base sets record.message = record.getMessage() (full!). Wait — base Formatter.format does `record.message = record.getMessage()`, overwriting my truncated assignment! Let me check CPython:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        record.message = record.getMessage()\n        if self.usesTime():\n            record.asctime = self.formatTime(record, self.datefmt)\n        s = self.formatMessage(record)\n        ...\n```\n\nYes — base format always sets record.message from msg/args. So temporarily setting record.message doesn't work.\n\nOptions: temporarily set record.msg = truncated and record.args = () ... but that mutates msg/args (restored after). Task says \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args、exc_info、stack_info\" — presumably means permanently; a restore is arguably ok but risky if a test inspects during? Tests check after formatting. Still risky wording.\n\nBetter: use a shallow copy of record. `copy.copy(record)` — LogRecord supports copy? It has no __slots__, so shallow copy works. Then set copy.message? No—base format sets it from msg/args, so also full. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: instead of relying on base format's Message, override formatMessage path: implement formatting of the message ourselves:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        truncated = ...\n        if truncated is None:\n            text = super().format(record)   # normal path\n        else:\n            text = self._format_with_message(record, truncated)\n```\n\nwhere _format_with_message builds the string itself:\n\n```python\n    def _format_with_message(self, record, message):\n        record.message = message\n        record.asctime = ... \n```\n\nHmm still touches record.message — that's fine, message is not in the forbidden list and base formatter sets it anyway.\n\nSimplest correct approach: manipulate msg/args on a copy via copy.copy, then let base format compute record.getMessage() from copy.msg = truncated, copy.args = (). Then copy.message etc all set on the copy. Original record untouched entirely. Need `import copy` in logger.py — allowed (\"可增加必要标准库导入\").\n\nWait — copy.copy of LogRecord: LogRecord defines __reduce__ ... Actually LogRecord has `__reduce__`? Let's think: logging.LogRecord defines `__init__`, `getMessage`, `__repr__`, and `__reduce__`? In CPython, LogRecord defines `__reduce__`:\n\n```python\n    def __reduce__(self):\n        import logging.handlers  # no, that's in handlers\n```\n\nHmm, in logging/__init__.py LogRecord:\n```python\n    def __reduce__(self):\n        return (self.__class__, (self.name, self.levelno,\n            self.pathname, self.lineno, self.msg, self.args,\n            self.exc_info, self.func, self.stack_info, self.extra? ))\n```\nNot sure. Let me check: Python 3.12 logging LogRecord has `__reduce__`? I recall `logging.Logger` pickling of LogRecord uses `__reduce__` defined in LogRecord:\n\n```python\n    def __reduce__(self):\n        return (self.__class__, (\n            self.name, self.levelno,\n            self.pathname, self.lineno,\n            self.msg, self.args,\n            self.exc_info))\n```\nHmm actually I think that exists. copy.copy uses __reduce_ex__ which for objects with __reduce__ defined will use it... copy.copy → checks __copy__, then __reduce_ex__(4) which for normal objects uses copyreg.__newobj__ ... Actually copy.copy uses `cls.__reduce_ex__(4)` only for \"non-atomic\" objects? copy.copy implementation:\n\n```python\ndef copy(x):\n    cls = type(x)\n    copier = _copy_dispatch.get(cls)\n    if copier: return copier(x)\n    if issubclass(cls, type): ...\n    copier = getattr(cls, \"__copy__\", None)\n    if copier: return copier(x)\n    reductor = dispatch_table.get(cls)\n    if reductor: ...\n    else:\n        reductor = getattr(x, \"__reduce_ex__\", None)\n        if reductor is not None:\n            rv = reductor(4)\n        else: ...\n```\nSo it uses __reduce_ex__(4). For a LogRecord without __reduce__, default object reduce → __dict__ copy, fine. If LogRecord defines __reduce__ with args, the reconstructed record's msg/args from original but other attrs (extra) lost? That could break keep_full_message detection on copy... but we detect before. Still, risky.\n\nSafer approach without copy: build the formatted string directly using base Formatter machinery by setting record.message... no.\n\nAlternative approach: use a tiny subclass-free trick: compute the first line via formatMessage ourselves? Actually we can bypass by formatting the \"prefix + truncated message\" manually:\n\n```python\n    def _format_with_truncated(self, record, message):\n        # 复制时间等属性但不改动 msg/args\n        saved_message = ...\n```\n\nHmm. Another clean approach: temporarily set record.msg = truncated, record.args = () and restore in finally. Tests likely check that after formatting, record.msg and record.args unchanged — restore satisfies that. The prohibition is about permanent rewriting. Both approaches are restores.\n\nI think the cleanest, least hacky: use `copy.copy`? Let me test what copy.copy does on LogRecord in the sandbox — I can run python. Let's just test.\n\nActually there's an even cleaner approach: override `formatMessage`? No, base format sets record.message = record.getMessage() before calling formatMessage, and formatMessage uses record.message. So the message comes from getMessage().\n\nBut we can override `format` to compute the truncated message and use `self.formatMessage` with a record whose message we set... but base format called by us — we could avoid calling base format entirely and replicate it:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        record.message = record.getMessage()  # base formatter does this too\n        truncated = ...\n        if truncated is not None:\n            record.message = truncated\n        if self.usesTime():\n            record.asctime = self.formatTime(record, self.datefmt)\n        msg = self.formatMessage(record)\n        if record.exc_info:\n            if not record.exc_text:\n                record.exc_text = self.formatException(record.exc_info)\n        if record.exc_text:\n            ...\n        if record.stack_info:\n            ...\n```\n\nThat replicates base; more code duplication but exact and doesn't touch msg/args/exc_info. But duplicating exception handling logic is error-prone (exc_text caching nuance: base does `if record.exc_info: if not record.exc_text: record.exc_text = self.formatException(...)`).\n\nHmm — note: base sets record.message = record.getMessage() and never restores. So record.message is left as full message by the base formatter anyway. So leaving record.message = truncated is not worse in terms of mutation category (message is derived). So the simple approach:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        keep_full = getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True\n        message_saved = missing\n        if budget is not None and not keep_full:\n            full = record.getMessage()\n            trunc = _truncate_message(full, budget)\n            if trunc != full:\n                # 不触碰 msg/args：临时替换派生属性 message\n                ...\n```\n\nbut base overwrites. So instead: temporarily replace msg/args? no.\n\nOK decide: replicate base format's message handling by temporarily swapping `record.msg` and `record.args`? Nope.\n\nCleanest: temporarily swap `record.getMessage`? Can't set attribute on instance... Actually you CAN set instance attribute `record.getMessage = lambda: trunc`? LogRecord instances allow attribute assignment (they have __dict__). Setting `record.getMessage` shadows the class method. Then base format's `record.message = record.getMessage()` gives truncated. Then delete the instance attribute in finally. That doesn't touch msg/args/exc_info at all!\n\nBut careful: base format also uses record.getMessage? Only for record.message. And our alignment uses record.message. Also exc_info formatting unaffected.\n\nHmm, but is getMessage allowed to be shadowed? `record.getMessage = ...` — setting an attribute named getMessage on instance works since LogRecord has __dict__ (no __slots__). But logging's extra handling: we're not passing extra. Fine.\n\nYet subtle: does anything cache getMessage? No.\n\nAlternatively, simplest and clearly acceptable: compute the formatted output with the truncated message by constructing the string from the prefix:\n\nmsg_full = super().format(record) → returns prefix + full message + optional exc text. Then I could find record.message in msg_full and replace it with truncated... but the newline-prefix alignment operates on the whole msg. Hmm: Actually truncation then reconstruct: msg = msg_full.replace(record.message, truncated, 1)? But exc_text may contain record.message? Unlikely but the last occurrence... Risky with %-format when message appears in exception text.\n\nI'll go with the getMessage shadow approach? It's clever but might look hacky; still it's clean semantically. Alternatively use `logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)` — that creates a new LogRecord from dict! `logging.makeLogRecord` copies __dict__ into a new LogRecord; then set copy.msg = truncated, copy.args = (), then super().format(copy). Original untouched. makeLogRecord:\n\n```python\ndef makeLogRecord(dict):\n    rv = LogRecord(None, None, \"\", 0, \"\", (), None, None)\n    rv.__dict__.update(dict)\n    return rv\n```\n\nBut careful: dict includes 'message' possibly (if previous handler set it) — and it includes exc_text maybe. Setting rv.msg = truncated, rv.args = () and rv.pop('message') — then base format recomputes. That's clean and uses only the logging module. But exc_text caching: if the full handler ran first and cached exc_text, copy carries it and reuses — same output. Fine.\n\nHowever one nuance: `record.__dict__` includes 'exc_info' possibly tuple with traceback - fine.\n\nI'll use makeLogRecord? Hmm, it requires dict copy; `logging.makeLogRecord(dict(record.__dict__))`. Both work. I prefer the getMessage-shadow? Let's think about which is more robust for the course's integration test: it formats with a StreamHandler capturing output, and then checks record attributes intact. Both fine.\n\nI'll use makeLogRecord approach — explicit and no attribute trickery. Wait but the test may check that the subsequent full handler output equals full message: yes, original record unchanged → full.\n\nHmm, one risk: makeLogRecord with 'messa